---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How to Scan a QR Code on iPhone (2026 Guide)"
  description="Learn how to scan QR codes on iPhone using the Camera app, Control Center, or Photos app. Step-by-step guide for iOS 17 and iOS 18."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">Published: October 5, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
        How to Scan a QR Code on iPhone (2026 Guide)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
        You don't need a third-party app to scan QR codes on iPhone. iOS has built-in
        scanners. Here are three ways to use them.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        QR codes are everywhere — restaurant menus, business cards, product packaging, and
        even TV ads. If you're using an iPhone, you can scan them without installing any
        app. iOS has had a built-in QR scanner since iOS 11.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Method 1: Camera App (Easiest)</h2>
      <p>
        This is the simplest method and works on almost every iPhone:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Open the <strong>Camera</strong> app on your iPhone.</li>
        <li>Point your camera at the QR code.</li>
        <li>Hold steady for 1–2 seconds.</li>
        <li>A yellow notification banner will appear at the top of the screen.</li>
        <li>Tap the banner to open the QR code's link or content.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <strong>Tip:</strong> Make sure the QR code is well-lit and fully visible in the
        frame. If it's too close or too far, the camera may not detect it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Method 2: Control Center Code Scanner (Fastest)</h2>
      <p>
        If you scan QR codes often, add the Code Scanner to Control Center for instant
        access:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Open <strong>Settings</strong> → <strong>Control Center</strong>.</li>
        <li>Find <strong>Code Scanner</strong> in the "More Controls" list.</li>
        <li>Tap the green <strong>+</strong> button to add it.</li>
        <li>Swipe down from the top-right corner to open Control Center.</li>
        <li>Tap the <strong>Code Scanner</strong> icon.</li>
        <li>Point at the QR code — it will scan automatically.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        This method is faster because it opens the scanner directly, without needing the
        Camera app.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Method 3: Photos App (For Saved Images)</h2>
      <p>
        If someone sent you a screenshot containing a QR code, you can scan it from Photos:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Open the <strong>Photos</strong> app.</li>
        <li>Open the image containing the QR code.</li>
        <li>Tap and hold on the QR code.</li>
        <li>A menu will appear with the QR code's link or content.</li>
        <li>Tap it to open.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        This uses Apple's <strong>Live Text</strong> feature, which works on iOS 15 and
        later.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Why Your iPhone Won't Scan a QR Code</h2>
      <p>
        If your iPhone isn't detecting the QR code, try these fixes:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Clean your camera lens</strong> — smudges often block detection.</li>
        <li><strong>Improve lighting</strong> — QR codes need even light, not glare.</li>
        <li><strong>Move closer or farther</strong> — the code should fill about 60% of the frame.</li>
        <li><strong>Hold steady</strong> — shaky hands can delay detection.</li>
        <li><strong>Check Camera settings</strong> — go to Settings → Camera and make sure
        "Scan QR Codes" is enabled (iOS 15+).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">⚠️ QR Code Safety: What to Watch For</h2>
      <p>
        QR codes can be used for phishing. Before scanning an unknown QR code:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Check the URL preview that appears before tapping.</li>
        <li>Avoid scanning QR codes stuck over existing ones (a common scam).</li>
        <li>Never enter passwords or payment details on a page opened from a random QR code.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Create Your Own QR Code</h2>
      <p>
        Want to make a QR code for your business, event, or website? Use our{' '}
        <a href="/tools/qr-generator" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium">
          free QR Code Generator
        </a>{' '}
        — enter any text or URL, choose a size, and download the PNG. No signup, no watermark.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Summary</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Camera app:</strong> Just point and tap the yellow banner.</li>
        <li><strong>Control Center:</strong> Add Code Scanner for instant access.</li>
        <li><strong>Photos app:</strong> Tap and hold a QR code in any image.</li>
        <li>No third-party app needed.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Scanning QR codes on iPhone is quick and built-in. And if you need to{' '}
        <a href="/tools/qr-generator" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium">
          create a QR code
        </a>
        , our free tool does it in seconds.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
      <a href="/blog" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium">
        ← Back to Blog
      </a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Layout>